# Install Prometheus and Grafana
# First, add the Prometheus community Helm repository:

helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts

helm repo update
# Now install the complete monitoring stack:
helm upgrade --install monitoring prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \
  --namespace monitoring \
  --set grafana.service.type=ClusterIP \
  --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.retention=7d

This installation includes:
Component	Purpose
Prometheus	Collects and stores metrics
Grafana	Displays monitoring dashboards
Alertmanager	Handles alerts
Node Exporter	Collects Linux node metrics
kube-state-metrics	Monitors Kubernetes resource states
Prometheus Operator	Manages Prometheus configurations


kubectl get pods -n monitoring
kubectl get svc -n monitoring

You should find services similar to:
- monitoring-grafana
- monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus
- monitoring-kube-prometheus-alertmanager


Access Grafana
=================
kubectl port-forward svc/monitoring-grafana 3000:80 -n monitoring

Grafana: http://localhost:3000

Username: admin
Password: prom-operator

Access Prometheus
=================
kubectl port-forward svc/monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus 9090:9090 -n monitoring

Prometheus: http://localhost:9090


========= Phase 3 is completed

Phase 4 – Cluster and infrastructure monitoring

Configure and understand:
- Node CPU utilization.
- Node memory utilization.
- Node disk utilization.
- Pod CPU consumption.
- Pod memory consumption.
- Pod restart count.
- Pod status.
- Deployment availability.
- Kubernetes resource utilization.



Phase 5 – Application and API monitoring
- Backend application metrics.
- HTTP request count.
- API response time.
- HTTP 4xx and 5xx errors.
- Request throughput.
- Database metrics if we add a database later.
- Service and Ingress metrics.

Phase 6 – Grafana dashboards and alerts

Create dashboards for:
- EKS cluster overview.
- Node monitoring.
- Pod monitoring.
- Application monitoring.
- API performance.
Configure alerts for high CPU, high memory, pod restarts and API failures.



Phase 7 – Monitoring experiments

Intentionally create problems:
- Increase CPU usage.
- Consume excessive memory.
- Kill a pod.
- Stop a deployment.
- Generate 500 API errors.
- Generate high API traffic.
Observe how Prometheus detects the problems and how Grafana displays them.

. Important: What Prometheus components actually monitor
I want you to understand these components rather than simply installing Helm charts.
Component	What it monitors
Prometheus Server	Scrapes and stores metrics
Node Exporter	Linux EC2 worker-node metrics
kube-state-metrics	Kubernetes object states
Kubelet / cAdvisor	Container resource usage
Prometheus Operator	Manages Prometheus configuration
Alertmanager	Routes and manages alerts
Grafana	Dashboards and visualization